window.YD_BUNDLE = {"research/01-who-gets-to-leave-home.md": "---\ntitle: \"Who Gets to *Leave Home?*\"\nslug: who-gets-to-leave-home\ntype: research\nnumber: 1\nseries: \"Policy Brief #01\"\ndate: 2026-09-01\ntags: [\"Housing\", \"Independence\", \"Europe\"]\ndescription: \"The global struggle for affordable youth housing — and what different countries are doing about it.\"\nread: \"22 min\"\nart: house\nstat:\n  value: \"26.3\"\n  unit: \"yrs\"\n  label: \"average age young Europeans leave the parental home\"\n  source: \"Eurostat (yth_demo_030), 2025\"\nquestions:\n  - icon: house\n    text: \"Why do young people in some countries achieve independent living much earlier than others?\"\n  - icon: bars\n    text: \"Which housing policies appear to reduce the barriers to independence?\"\n  - icon: globe\n    text: \"What does each approach actually give young people — and what does it cost them, their families, taxpayers, or the wider housing market?\"\n---\n\nAcross Europe, the age at which young people leave home varies by roughly a decade — from the early twenties in the Nordic countries to over thirty in parts of the south and east. Income explains some of that gap. Policy explains a surprising amount of the rest. This brief compares how different countries help — or fail to help — young people take the first step into a home of their own.\n\n## Key findings\n\n1. Countries with large social, cooperative or non-profit rental sectors see earlier independence, even where youth incomes are lower.\n2. Direct cash subsidies for young renters help individuals, but can push up prices where housing supply is fixed.\n3. The most effective packages pair supply — homes young people can actually rent — with security of tenure once they are in.\n\n## In their words\n\n> I have a job, a degree and a partner. What I don’t have is a door with my own name on it.\n>\n> — Marta, 29\n\n## Method\n\nComparative review of national and city-level policy, triangulated with published statistics and interviews with young people in each country studied.\n\n**Framework:** Problem → People → Policy → Proof → Lessons\n", "data-lab/01-age-of-independence.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Age of Independence\"\nslug: age-of-independence\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 1\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Housing\", \"Europe\", \"Gender\"]\ndescription: \"At what age do young Europeans leave the parental home — and how much does it differ between countries, over time, and between women and men?\"\ndataset: independence\nart: house\n---\n\n{{ answer independence }}\n\n{{ figure independence/gender }}\n\n{{ figure independence/trend }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Eurostat estimates the average age at which young people leave the parental household from the EU Labour Force Survey, using the share of young people living with their parents at each age.\n- The indicator is an estimate of a typical age, not an observed average of individual moves.\n", "data-lab/02-cost-of-being-young.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Cost of Being Young\"\nslug: cost-of-being-young\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 2\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Housing\", \"Europe\", \"Cost of living\"]\ndescription: \"How many young people spend more than 40% of their income just on housing — and how does that compare with everyone else?\"\ndataset: cost\nart: rooms\n---\n\n{{ answer cost }}\n\n{{ figure cost/compare }}\n\n{{ figure cost/trend }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Housing cost overburden rate: the share of people living in households where total housing costs (net of housing allowances) represent more than 40% of disposable income (net of housing allowances).\n- Eurostat does not publish harmonised cross-country data on other living costs (food, transport) broken down by the age of the person, so this post measures housing costs only.\n", "data-lab/03-first-paycheck.md": "---\ntitle: \"The First Paycheck\"\nslug: first-paycheck\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 3\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Work\", \"Europe\", \"Wages\"]\ndescription: \"How much do workers under 30 earn per hour across Europe once prices are taken into account — and how far behind the average are they?\"\ndataset: paycheck\nart: ladder\n---\n\n{{ answer paycheck }}\n\n{{ figure paycheck/compare }}\n\n{{ figure paycheck/ratio }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- The Structure of Earnings Survey (SES) is run every four years; 2022 is the latest wave. It covers enterprises with at least 10 employees and excludes public administration (NACE section O).\n- Mean gross hourly earnings include overtime pay. PPS is an artificial currency that equalises purchasing power across countries.\n", "data-lab/04-where-young-people-are-going.md": "---\ntitle: \"Where Young People Are Going\"\nslug: where-young-people-are-going\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 4\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Mobility\", \"Europe\", \"Migration\"]\ndescription: \"At what age do people move between countries — and which European countries gain or lose young adults through migration?\"\ndataset: migration\nart: globe\n---\n\n{{ answer migration }}\n\n{{ figure migration/age }}\n\n{{ figure migration/net }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Immigration and emigration count people changing their usual residence for at least 12 months, including nationals returning or leaving.\n- Emigration is typically harder to record than immigration (people do not always deregister), so net figures may overstate inflows. Some countries record age in completed years and others as age reached during the year; each country is used under the definition it reports.\n", "data-lab/05-youth-jobs-map.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Youth Jobs Map\"\nslug: youth-jobs-map\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 5\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Work\", \"Global\", \"Employment\"]\ndescription: \"Where in the world do young people find work — and where are they locked out of jobs, education and training?\"\ndataset: jobs\nart: grid\n---\n\n{{ answer jobs }}\n\n{{ figure jobs/map }}\n\n{{ figure jobs/trend }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Unemployment and participation are ILO modelled estimates, which fill gaps in national data to produce comparable series for all countries. Recent years are estimates and are revised as survey data arrive.\n- Youth unemployment is measured against the labour force (those working or looking for work), not the whole youth population; students not seeking work are outside the labour force. NEET is measured against the whole population aged 15–24.\n", "data-lab/06-generation-gap.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Generation Gap\"\nslug: generation-gap\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 6\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Society\", \"Europe\", \"Poverty\", \"Income\"]\ndescription: \"Are young adults or older people more likely to be poor — and how does their income compare?\"\ndataset: gap\nart: queue\n---\n\n{{ answer gap }}\n\n{{ figure gap/poverty }}\n\n{{ figure gap/trend }}\n\n{{ figure gap/income }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- At-risk-of-poverty rate: share of people whose equivalised disposable income (after social transfers) is below 60% of the national median. It measures low income relative to others in the same country, not absolute deprivation.\n- Income refers to the household a person lives in; many 18–24-year-olds still live with their parents and share their household income.\n", "data-lab/07-education-to-work.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Education-to-Work Journey\"\nslug: education-to-work\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 7\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Education\", \"Europe\", \"Employment\"]\ndescription: \"How likely are young people to be in work one to three years after leaving education — and how much does the kind of education matter?\"\ndataset: eduwork\nart: book\n---\n\n{{ answer eduwork }}\n\n{{ figure eduwork/levels }}\n\n{{ figure eduwork/vocational }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- The indicator covers 20–34-year-olds who are no longer in education or training and who completed their highest level of education one to three years earlier.\n- Eurostat flags some estimates as having low reliability (small samples); these are shown faded and marked in the table.\n", "data-lab/08-a-day-in-the-life.md": "---\ntitle: \"A Day in the Life\"\nslug: a-day-in-the-life\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 8\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Society\", \"Europe\", \"Time use\"]\ndescription: \"How do 15–24-year-olds spend their 24 hours — and how does their day differ from everyone else’s?\"\ndataset: day\nart: clock\n---\n\n{{ answer day }}\n\n{{ figure day/24h }}\n\n{{ figure day/diff }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Time-use surveys ask respondents to keep a diary of their activities in 10-minute slots. Figures are averages over all respondents in the group and over all days of the week.\n- The 2020 wave of the Harmonised European Time Use Surveys (HETUS) covers the countries shown; fieldwork dates differ by country.\n", "data-lab/09-youth-population-map.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Youth Population Map\"\nslug: youth-population-map\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 9\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Society\", \"Global\", \"Demography\"]\ndescription: \"Where is the population of 15–24-year-olds growing, and where is it shrinking, between now and 2050?\"\ndataset: population\nart: town\n---\n\n{{ answer population }}\n\n{{ figure population/map }}\n\n{{ figure population/regions }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Population aged 15–24 is the sum of the male and female 15–19 and 20–24 age groups. Projections are the UN World Population Prospects medium variant, as published by the World Bank.\n- Projections are not forecasts: they depend on assumptions about fertility, mortality and migration.\n", "data-lab/10-digital-generation.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Digital Generation\"\nslug: digital-generation\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 10\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Technology\", \"Europe\", \"AI\", \"Digital skills\"]\ndescription: \"How many young people use generative AI and have basic digital skills — and how far ahead of older adults are they?\"\ndataset: digital\nart: phone\n---\n\n{{ answer digital }}\n\n{{ figure digital/ai }}\n\n{{ figure digital/purposes }}\n\n{{ figure digital/skills }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Data come from the EU survey on ICT usage in households and by individuals. “All” refers to individuals aged 16–74.\n- Overall digital skills combine five areas (information, communication, content creation, safety and problem solving); “basic or above” means at least basic in all five.\n"};
